jordan peele's nope left some audiences  saying hell yeah others however were  ready to get out of theaters  the opening scene of nope is a  viscerally horrifying experience that  puts viewers on edge while setting up  the themes of the film all at the same  time it's almost entirely quiet and  comprised of a single simple shot a  chimp and a birthday hat covered in  blood on the set of a television show  it's clear that the chimp has run amuck  and savagely mauled the girl who may or  may not be dead chimp attacks on their  own are terrifying but coming at the  very start of a film supposedly about  aliens it's as upsetting as can be one  reddit user wrote it's truly the  scariest scene in my opinion in this  movie it makes you want to look away but  the feeling of needing to know what  happens next is curiosity taking over  following the opening scene we meet otis  haywood senior and his son oj  unfortunately the haywood patriarch is  badly wounded by falling debris he  abruptly stops moving and sags atop the  horse he is riding after he's rushed to  the hospital we find out that a nickel  fell from the sky through otis senior's  head and fatally embedded itself in his  brain while that would be upsetting  enough some fans have picked up on a  more insidious part of the scene as one  reddit user points out the overall theme  of nope is trying to profit off things  you really probably shouldn't profit  from what kills keith david's character  is money falling from the sky  for the sheer number of comments it's  obvious that one of the most upsetting  sequences in nope is when the saucer  sucks up the crowd at jup's show the  scene itself is gruesome but the  implications of what comes next are even  worse the saucer is no alien aircraft  but rather an animal itself which means  that the crowd is actually being  digested instead of just abducted an  exchange between two reddit users sums  up the scene's implications well so what  they're being slowly digested while  suspended in air for hours i guess until  it blenders them for something  sporadically throughout nope chillingly  vague screams echo throughout the canyon  usually when the saucer is in sight the  movie spends a long time sprinkling the  screams throughout before it ever even  hints at an explanation on top of that  the screams seem to come from multiple  beings at once sounding like any number  and combination of people and animals  even before the spine-chilling sounds  are explained they managed to upset fans  to their core one reddit user was one of  the first to cite the pervasive screams  as one of the most upsetting parts of  nope saying that their screams from the  clouds was horrible too and then  suddenly cut off chills the screams make  the digestion scene even worse implying  that the saucer carries its victims in  its stomach for days slowly and  painfully digesting them one fan writes  i'd never seen that in a monster movie  before the monster's victim still alive  inside it and screaming giving you a  sense of the bean's location but in a  way that is more terrifying than useful  and then it swallows them  we are making something new and special  and something that is a testament to why  i love movies  and of course the digestion scene leads  directly to one of the most horrifying  moments in the entire film the  bloodrayne scene the scene which is  already being compared to the infamous  elevator blood scene from the shining  sees em and angel trapped in the ranch  house overnight the saucer menacingly  hovering directly over them like we had  seen before small metal objects begin  falling from the sky clinking against  the house's walls and windows then the  rain switches to a torrent of dark red  blood which repaints the formerly white  building red for the rest of the movie  yeah they repainted your house as we  gradually come to realize the blood in  all the metal objects that had ever  fallen from the sky were actually the  saucer creature expelling its waste one  user wrote that we see the jean jacket  vomit the objects it cannot digest then  you wonder what's next will it destroy  the house attempting to kill em and  angel nope blood starts to slowly pour  in a terrifying shininesque sequence  we already covered the opening scene  which jumpstarts fan terror with images  of a bloody gorty in his victim but nope  has much more gorty violence to parade  out later in the movie after some  balloons unexpectedly pop on set the  chimp burst into a murderous rage  brutally mauling his co-stars the one  exception is a young juke who gordy  leaves unharmed the sequence did not sit  well with fans at all one reddit user  wrote that the entire scene was so  nerve-wracking especially when he sees  young juke under the table and just  stares into the camera my anxiety was so  high while another fan wrote the gordy  scene was masterfully done and stuck  with me as truly unsettling  the vast majority of nope moments that  upset fans were pretty purposefully  meant to invite discussion they either  delivered on the film's promise of  horror or delivered on its social  commentary in both cases nailing that  sweet spot so typical appeals work but a  few of the upsetting moments seem to be  drawing such ire from viewers because  the suspension of disbelief is being  broken case in point the praying mantis  which has already begun to generate its  own discussion threads for its absurdity  and convenience to the plot the insect  in question appears at the exact moment  the haywoods try to film the saucer it  covers a digital camera that angel  prepared beforehand throughout the  entire alien encounter and flies away of  its own record the moment the saucer  leaves preventing any useful recording  as one reddit user commented the use of  the mantas felt weak and flimsy their  setup would have worked fine if not for  the praying mantis though and they  abandoned that setup after one try  they're right given that oj specifically  comments on how rare the bugs are in  that area making the moments even more  frustrating and though other users  considered the insect's presence just  another bad miracle the sheer  coincidence of the insect being such an  obstacle to the haywood's success  doesn't make it any less upsetting that  said it's worth noting that peel likely  meant for this moment to be upsetting to  fans on purpose the scene becomes even  more suspenseful not just because of the  danger that the saucer poses to oj but  because it's up to emerald to try to  remove the insect in time to capture the  saucer on video if fans were upset that  the haywoods didn't succeed when the  movie wasn't even half over that just  shows that they were invested in  characters  to some fans the choices and actions of  antlers holst may be the most annoying  parts of nope the character is superbly  played by michael wincott is presented  throughout the film as a true master of  his craft he's an old school guerrilla  filmmaker and an incredibly talented  cinematographer he chooses his projects  carefully and it's clear that he values  integrity and intelligence over all else  in his career that's why it's so  aggravating when holst agrees to help  the haywards film the saucer and  seemingly blows it the supposedly expert  filmmaker decides to take both himself  and his never-before-seen world-changing  footage of the saucer creature and  voluntarily feed himself and it to the  saucer after all this time trying to  capture the perfect shot of the creature  he succeeds but only by throwing his own  life away the act has prompted a number  of dedicated discussion threads such as  what was up with antler holst and the  climax my theory about holston is  sacrifice and the most direct of all  what antlers holst did was very stupid  can you guys explain why he would have  done that  with all of the intersecting messages  and nope centered around the dangers of  dehumanization disrespecting living  beings trying to control predators and  putting spectacle ahead of empathy some  inattentive viewers think it was not  choice for the movie to end with the  creature murdered by m to these fans the  scenes involving gordy and his effect on  juke's later life were meant as examples  of what not to do those who try to  dominate dangerous animals and harm them  for selfish gain risk violent  retribution and moral compromise the  same is seemingly true for the scenes  involving oj and his horse lucky and how  the pair were treated on a commercial  set the theme supposedly carries into  the third act as the cast prepares to  get the perfect shot of the creature  however one fan believes that the third  act actually leads to oj taming the  creature that was jean jacket showing  respect to oj oj challenged it in one  and after that revealed its true self to  him seeing him as an equal and let him  live another fan added that respect is  given shown and taken in most animal  species it's not a human specific  characteristic these fans seem to  believe that the scene featured a  genuine bond between oj and jean jacket  which would make the creature's death a  confusing choice of course considering  that jean jacket is directly responsible  for the death of o.j and hems father on  top of being a bloodthirsty killer of  both people and horses it may be safe to  say that gene jacket's comfort wasn't  exactly the top priority for the  haywoods  by its end nope reveals itself to be  anything but the alien movie most  viewers expected what is initially  believed to be a spaceship containing  alien visitors from another planet turns  out to be an organism itself a massive  flying predator that sucks people and  animals into its saucer-like body to eat  them not probe them sorry angel  they're just waiting for the perfect  time to shove metal probes up our asses  and while the film's characters still  seem to believe the creature is alien in  origin there is not necessarily anything  in the film to suggest that the creature  is anything but a previously  undiscovered species of animal this  twist turning an alien invasion movie  into a creature feature is playful and  fresh that doesn't stop fans of alien  invasion movies from being disappointed  he'll certainly build the movie as an  alien movie even citing the recent  release of previously unseen government  footage of ufos is an inspiration  however the lack of explicit  confirmation within the world of the  film seems to have angered some viewers  [Music]

